WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.560
Which "Fast and Furious" film almost destroyed&nbsp;
the entire franchise because it didn't have enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.640
stars? Keep watching to find out about&nbsp;
the most disastrous sequels in history!

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:14.800
For a solid decade from 2002 to 2012, Will&nbsp;
Smith could do no wrong at the box office.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.360
Smith did it largely without relying on sequels,&nbsp;
headlining ten hits that were star vehicles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.800
including risky big-budget movies&nbsp;
like "I, Robot" and "Hancock."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.920
However, Smith's "butts-in-seats" drawing power&nbsp;
started to slide with 2013's "After Earth."

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:30.880
When most movie stars' careers start&nbsp;
to slide, they hit the panic button&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:34.560
and go back to the franchises that made them&nbsp;
famous in the first place — and sure enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.120
Smith went back for a third "Bad Boys" film in&nbsp;
2020. But as a movie star, he really got his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:45.280
start with "Independence Day," a 1996 surprise&nbsp;
hit that brought in $817 million worldwide.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.840
So why wouldn't Smith star in its 2016&nbsp;
sequel "Independence Day Resurgence"?

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:54.160
According to Smith, he wanted to do "Suicide&nbsp;
Squad" instead. So, Fox plowed ahead with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.880
sequel without their biggest star, even if actors&nbsp;
like Jeff Goldblum and Bill Pullman did return.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:04.080
The film bombed with $384 million&nbsp;
worldwide on a $165 million budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:09.440
while "Suicide Squad" nearly doubled that&nbsp;
with $746 million worldwide. As it turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:13.440
moviegoers didn't just want to see evil aliens;&nbsp;
they wanted to see Will Smith punching them.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.040
A common cliche in movie fan culture is:&nbsp;
"Nobody cares about the humans in a monster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:23.680
movie." So nobody cared about Ripley in&nbsp;
"Alien?" Or Ann Darrow in "King Kong?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.320
Yes, you go to a monster movie for the monsters,&nbsp;
but you care about it because of the characters.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:33.040
A good case in point is 2014's "Godzilla."&nbsp;
"Godzilla's" trailer was one of the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:38.240
of the past 20 years, headlining a marketing&nbsp;
campaign that put Bryan Cranston front and center.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:43.520
Now, Cranston isn't necessarily an A-list movie&nbsp;
star, but in 2014 he was just coming off his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.560
career-defining work in "Breaking Bad," so his&nbsp;
presence lent "Godzilla" serious credibility.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:52.560
However, while Cranston was marketed as the&nbsp;
lead, he was killed off in the first half-hour.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:56.560
As a result, Cranston wasn't in&nbsp;
"Godzilla: King of the Monsters" at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.760
Without any clear connection to the forgettable&nbsp;
2014 film, "Godzilla: King of the Monsters"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:09.760
opened to a tepid $47 million and ended its run at&nbsp;
$383 million worldwide on a $170 million budget.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:15.280
Not a bomb, but definitely a disappointment&nbsp;
compared with the original's $529 million. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:20.800
lack of Cranston wasn't the cause, but a symptom:&nbsp;
Moviegoers do care about humans in monster movies.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:25.360
There are some movie stars that audiences&nbsp;
simply enjoy watching in special effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.440
movies. Jim Carrey is different — he&nbsp;
is his own special effect. So making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:33.680
sequels to Jim Carrey movies without Jim&nbsp;
Carrey may be the most nonsensical thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:38.640
ever. Yet it happened not once, not&nbsp;
twice, but thrice: "Dumb and Dumberer:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:44.640
When Harry Met Lloyd" in 2003, "Son of the&nbsp;
Mask" in 2005, and "Evan Almighty" in 2007.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:46.720
Why no Carrey? He doesn't like sequels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:50.000
and if these were the scripts he was&nbsp;
being offered, it's easy to see why.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.440
"Dumb and Dumberer" face-planted first, earning&nbsp;
$28 million worldwide on a $30 million budget.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:59.440
You would think such a performance would put the&nbsp;
brakes on "Son of the Mask" and "Evan Almighty,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.480
but surprisingly "Son of the Mask" followed with&nbsp;
Jamie Kennedy in the lead, earning $59 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:10.240
worldwide on a mind-boggling $100 million budget.&nbsp;
Then "Evan Almighty" came out, with Universal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:16.640
spending an incredible $175 million son the Steve&nbsp;
Carrell vehicle to earn back... $174 million.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:21.120
When the smoke cleared, the studio had spent&nbsp;
more than double the "Bruce Almighty" budget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.520
on a Jim Carrey sequel without Jim&nbsp;
Carrey. That just defies explanation.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:34.400
"Do you want to explain yourself, congressman?&nbsp;
What is going on?" "Oh. These… are birds!"

00:03:35.985 --> 00:03:39.600
"Ghostbusters" was a genuine cultural&nbsp;
sensation in 1984, and while the film's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:45.360
1989 sequel isn't well-regarded, it was&nbsp;
still a huge deal, earning $215 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:50.880
worldwide. Then the franchise went dormant&nbsp;
for 27 years until it was rebooted in 2016.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:55.440
This reboot was simply called "Ghostbusters,"&nbsp;
but many folks referred to it as some variation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.600
of "The Girl Ghostbusters Movie," because&nbsp;
it had female leads, with Bill Murray, Dan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:04.720
Aykroyd and Ernie Hudson only appearing in brief&nbsp;
"torch-passing" cameos. But moviegoers who waited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:09.520
decades for another sequel didn't want to see any&nbsp;
torches being passed — they wanted the originals.

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:12.960
It also probably didn't help that online&nbsp;
chatter in the months leading up to the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:17.920
revolved around dumb, petty arguments about&nbsp;
whether women could/should be Ghostbusters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:21.280
It's rarely a good sign when instead of&nbsp;
talking about how excited people are to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:24.480
see your upcoming movie, they're&nbsp;
angrily debating such nonsense.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:29.440
Ultimately, despite the star power of Kristen&nbsp;
Wiig, Melissa McCarthy and Kate McKinnon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:36.000
the film closed with $229 million worldwide&nbsp;
($67 million less than "Ghostbusters" 32&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:40.720
years earlier). While many blamed sexism for&nbsp;
the movie's failure, another possible culprit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:43.920
was making a "Ghostbusters" movie&nbsp;
without the original Ghostbusters.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:46.880
"We came, we saw, we kicked its ass!"

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:54.320
Harrison Ford's box office earnings are $5.2&nbsp;
billion domestically and $10 billion worldwide.&nbsp;

00:04:54.320 --> 00:05:00.960
One of his many hits was 1993's "The Fugitive,"&nbsp;
which earned $183 million domestically and $353&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:05.520
million worldwide, finishing third at the&nbsp;
year-end box office. While Tommy Lee Jones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:10.480
won an Oscar for his role as U.S. Marshal Samuel&nbsp;
Gerard, it was Ford who put the butts in seats.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:14.240
So, perhaps "U.S. Marshals," a sequel&nbsp;
to "The Fugitive" focused on Gerard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:18.480
was doomed from the get-go because it lacked&nbsp;
the key ingredient to the original's popularity:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.960
Harrison Ford. The film did add in Wesley&nbsp;
Snipes and Robert Downey Jr. but "Marshals"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:28.240
failed to find an audience, earning $57&nbsp;
million on a $60 million budget in 1998.

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:32.240
While it doesn't seem like Ford should&nbsp;
have played the titular fugitive again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:36.320
it does seem clear that "U.S. Marshals" probably&nbsp;
shouldn't have been made in the first place.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:41.520
In 1997, Will Smith and Tommy Lee Jones&nbsp;
combined their star power with "Men In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:46.880
Black," which earned $587 million worldwide. And&nbsp;
while two "Men In Black" sequels failed to reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:51.280
the same pop culture heights, both made a lot&nbsp;
of money. "Men In Black 3" actually made more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:56.000
worldwide (thanks to the benefit of 15 years of&nbsp;
inflation and a bigger international market).

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:00.160
In 2019, though, Sony Pictures tried to&nbsp;
reboot the franchise without Smith or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:04.000
Jones, replacing them instead with&nbsp;
Chris Hemsworth and Tessa Thompson.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:10.160
"Men In Black: International" closed with $80&nbsp;
million domestically and $253 million worldwide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:16.640
$188 million less than the previous franchise&nbsp;
low, "Men In Black II", earned way back in 2002.

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:21.760
Thanks to a modest $110 million budget, "Men&nbsp;
In Black: International" probably squeaked by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:26.640
with a small profit, but the writing was on&nbsp;
the wall. No Smith, no Jones, no audience.

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:32.000
"The Fast and The Furious" franchise has been&nbsp;
a wild ride — on screen and behind-the-scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:36.240
as well. The franchise went from popular&nbsp;
and profitable in the early 2000s to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.240
billion-dollar blockbuster franchise&nbsp;
in the 2010s, all while charting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:45.120
character's perfectly natural progression from&nbsp;
LA street racers to international super spies.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:49.840
"The Fast and Furious" franchise does have&nbsp;
one black sheep, however: 2006's "The Fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:55.920
and The Furious: Tokyo Drift." Where this&nbsp;
prequel/sequel/spin-off fits into the overarching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.920 --> 00:07:02.160
"Furious" timeline is a topic for debate among&nbsp;
fans, but this much is clear: It crashed, badly.

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:08.960
On an $85 million budget, "Tokyo Drift" earned&nbsp;
only $62 million domestically and $157 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:14.080
worldwide. The impetus seems clear: it starred&nbsp;
none of the original cast, largely because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:18.400
stars like Vin Diesel and Paul Walker left&nbsp;
and were building their careers elsewhere.

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:22.800
"Drift" nearly killed the franchise — but a&nbsp;
closing scene-cameo from Diesel gave hope for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:27.360
a return. So, Universal got the band back&nbsp;
together for 2009's "Fast and Furious",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:30.480
saving the series and adding billions&nbsp;
of dollars to their bank account.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:33.360
The key takeaway? You can't break up the family.

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:39.040
It's one thing to make a sequel without the&nbsp;
original cast — but without the central premise?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:42.160
It'd be like making a "Jaws"&nbsp;
sequel without a shark.

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:47.600
Enter "Halloween III." "Halloween" invented the&nbsp;
modern slasher film in 1978, putting a face to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:53.360
invisible killer of 1974's "Black Christmas" with&nbsp;
a charismatic uber-baddie named Michael Myers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:57.360
Perhaps just as critical was Jamie Lee&nbsp;
Curtis as scream queen Laurie Strode and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:01.920
Donald Pleasance as Michael's haunted hunter&nbsp;
Sam Loomis. Together, the trio propelled the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:09.120
original "Halloween" to $70 million worldwide&nbsp;
on a $325,000 budget. While the $25 million of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:14.960
1981's "Halloween II" was a big drop-off,&nbsp;
on a $2.5 million budget it did just fine.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:18.960
But whe series creators John Carpenter and Debra&nbsp;
Hill were asked to put together a third film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:21.680
they agreed to participate only&nbsp;
if the "Halloween" name would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:25.440
be used as more of an anthology&nbsp;
title, with Myers being dropped.

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:29.440
Instead, "Halloween III: Season of the Witch"&nbsp;
is about an evil conglomerate that turns kids'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:34.080
heads into bugs when they wear a mask and&nbsp;
watch a TV show. While "Witch" made money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:39.920
($14 million on a $2.5 million budget in 1982),&nbsp;
it was such a departure that the series took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:45.920
a time out for six years. By 1988, Michael&nbsp;
Myers was back, and he has been ever since.

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:47.200
It should be mentioned, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:50.080
that despite "Season of the Witch"&nbsp;
underperforming at the box office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:55.200
it has gone on to become a creepy cult favorite —&nbsp;
in no small part because of its ominous, "London&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:59.280
Bridge is Falling Down"-inspired jingle from&nbsp;
the Silver Shamrock Halloween mask commercial.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:05.600
Despite its B-movie title, Paul Verhoeven's&nbsp;
sci-fi satire "RoboCop" earned $53 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:11.520
worldwide in 1987, as well as a reputation for&nbsp;
being one of the best action movies of the 1980s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:16.240
A big reason was the man wearing the robot suit:&nbsp;
Peter Weller. Weller came back for "RoboCop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:22.320
2," which earned $45 million in 1990, but was&nbsp;
over the franchise by the third film in 1993.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:26.960
As it turns out, moviegoers were too. With&nbsp;
Robert John Burke stepping into the suit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:30.640
"RoboCop 3" bombed with a&nbsp;
dreadful $10 million worldwide.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:36.800
This is despite (or maybe because of) "RoboCop&nbsp;
3" being rated PG-13, not R like the first two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:41.520
presumably because the filmmakers wanted to&nbsp;
expand the potential audience. So much for that.

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:46.480
While it's unfair to blame the "RoboCop 3" failure&nbsp;
on Burke replacing Weller, it's hard to deny that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:51.520
the drop between the first two films and the third&nbsp;
— both in quality and box-office — was gigantic.

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:54.320
"I'd buy that for a dollar!"

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:58.560
"The Terminator" made stars out of&nbsp;
filmmaker James Cameron and actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:02.880
Arnold Schwarzenegger. "Terminator 2:&nbsp;
Judgment Day" made them icons. Hollywood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:08.080
has been trying to recapture the magic ...&nbsp;
again and again and again ... ever since.

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:13.600
"The Terminator" was an out-of-nowhere sleeper&nbsp;
hit in 1984, earning $78 million worldwide on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:20.160
a $6 million budget. "Terminator 2" took a $100&nbsp;
million budget and turned it into a $515 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:25.280
blockbuster, beating even Disney's "Beauty &amp; The&nbsp;
Beast" to top the year-end box office charts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:28.640
In hindsight, maybe the franchise&nbsp;
should have thrown in the towel at that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:32.480
But there was more money to be made ...&nbsp;
if only the studio could figure out how.

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:37.040
After the mild success of 2003's "Rise&nbsp;
of the Machines," Warner Brothers tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:41.360
to relaunch the franchise without Arnold&nbsp;
at all in 2009 for "Terminator Salvation,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:46.240
investing a bafflingly high $200 million in&nbsp;
the sequel — and the most memorable thing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:50.640
come out of it was the controversy over Christian&nbsp;
Bale screaming at a crew member during filming.

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:58.720
The movie bombed with $125 million domestic and&nbsp;
$365 million worldwide. By 2015's "Terminator:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:03.840
Genisys" not even Arnold could save the franchise,&nbsp;
while "Terminator: Dark Fate" tanked worldwide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:09.200
despite reuniting Arnold, Linda Hamilton, and&nbsp;
producer James Cameron. Hasta la vista, baby.

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:15.680
If peak 1980s Arnold Schwarzenegger battling a&nbsp;
7-foot tall killer alien gets your blood pumping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:18.000
well, you're not alone. Moviegoers didn't "get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:22.240
to da choppa," but they did flock to&nbsp;
the theater in 1987 to see "Predator."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:28.560
On an $18 million budget, "Predator" earned $59&nbsp;
million domestically and $98 million worldwide.

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:32.960
However, rather than become the "Predator"&nbsp;
franchise's Lt. Ellen Ripley, Schwarzenegger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:38.560
opted out of the film's sequel. So who would&nbsp;
take his place? Stallone? Van Damme? Gibson?

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:40.160
Nope. Danny Glover.

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:43.600
Look, Glover's a fine actor and was&nbsp;
great in the "Lethal Weapon" franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:47.600
but he wasn't quite a credible replacement&nbsp;
for Schwarzenegger. Despite nearly doubling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:52.640
the original's budget to $35 million,&nbsp;
"Predator 2" managed a minuscule $28 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:56.320
domestically and $54 million worldwide. Ouch.

